Close the -ENOTDIR existence oracle at the choke point, for the whole family.

An -ENOTDIR proves a name EXISTS -- the walk stepped into something that is not
a directory. An absent name gives -ENOENT. Every path-resolving syscall that can
be made to want a directory is therefore a one-syscall existence probe of a
hidden path:

  stat(p "/zzz")                    chdir(p)
  stat(p "/")                       open(p, O_PATH|O_DIRECTORY)
  open(p, O_DIRECTORY)              inotify_add_watch(p, IN_ONLYDIR)

WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY

The two files this supersedes -- ghost_notdir.patch and ghost_notdir_5_10.patch
-- guarded ONE of those sites: link_path_walk()'s non-final-component
`d_can_lookup()` bail. That closed `stat(p "/zzz")` and nothing else. The other
five all reach -ENOTDIR through the LOOKUP_DIRECTORY test that link_path_walk()
never sees, so a complete one-syscall substitute for every oracle this whole
directory closes was still sitting there, unguarded, the entire time.

It also put a guard inside link_path_walk(), the hottest function in the VFS and
the one hunk in this patch set that carried a documented bootloop risk. That risk
is gone: link_path_walk() is no longer touched by _ghost at all.

And it needed two variants, because 5.10 spells the unlazy step unlazy_walk()
where 5.15+ spells it try_to_unlazy(). The choke points below do not care which,
so ONE file now covers 5.10 / 5.15 / 6.1 / 6.6 / 6.12 with byte-identical hunks.

*** THE FIRST HUNK USED TO LAND IN THE WRONG FUNCTION -- READ THIS ***

This is why the hunk carries FOUR lines of context and why the first of them is
`*path = nd->path;`. It is not style.

path_lookupat() and path_parentat() end with the SAME five lines:

	if (!err) {
		<assign>
		nd->path.mnt = NULL;
		nd->path.dentry = NULL;
	}
	terminate_walk(nd);
	return err;
}

The earlier revision of this file used three lines of leading context, starting
at `nd->path.mnt = NULL;`. That pre-image occurs TWICE in fs/namei.c on every one
of the five trees, and `patch` resolves an ambiguous pre-image by proximity to
the @@ line number -- which is pinned to 6.12. Measured, on the pinned OnePlus
trees, with `patch -p1 -F0` and no fuzz reported on any of them:

	5.10   guard landed in path_parentat()   <- wrong
	5.15   guard landed in path_parentat()   <- wrong
	6.1    guard landed in path_parentat()   <- wrong
	6.6    guard landed in path_parentat()   <- wrong
	6.12   guard landed in path_lookupat()      correct

So on four of the five fleet kernels the ENOTDIR family was NOT closed at all --
the guard sat on the parent-lookup path instead, where it rewrote an errno for
unlink/rename/mkdir and left stat(p "/zzz"), stat(p "/"), chdir(p),
open(O_PATH|O_DIRECTORY) and inotify IN_ONLYDIR wide open. Nothing reported it:
the patch applied at fuzz 0, the build compiled, and every existing assertion
("fs/namei.c contains ghost_hidden_path") passed.

`*path = nd->path;` is the one line that differs between the two functions --
path_parentat() writes `*parent = nd->path;` -- so including it makes the
pre-image unique. Verified by counting occurrences in all five trees: 2 without
it, 1 with it. `scripts/apply_nomount_stack.sh verify ghost` now asserts the
guard is inside path_lookupat() by name, so this cannot regress silently again.

THE LESSON, STATED FOR THE NEXT PATCH IN THIS DIRECTORY: fuzz 0 is necessary and
NOT sufficient. A hunk whose pre-image appears more than once in the target file
lands wherever the line numbers say, and the line numbers are pinned to one tree.
Count your pre-image before you trust the dry-run.

TWO SITES, NOT ONE

path_lookupat() is the choke point for everything that is not an open(2):
link_path_walk()'s -ENOTDIR and the LOOKUP_DIRECTORY test both land in the same
`err` before terminate_walk(), so one guard closes stat/chdir/inotify and
open(O_PATH|O_DIRECTORY) together.

do_open() is the second site and is NOT reachable from the first: path_openat()
runs link_path_walk() and open_last_lookups() itself rather than calling
path_lookupat(), and its LOOKUP_DIRECTORY test sits ahead of may_open(), so the
engine's hijacked ->permission -- which is what answers -ENOENT for a plain
open(2) -- never gets a turn. Guarding only path_lookupat() would leave
`open(p, O_DIRECTORY)` as a complete replacement for all six probes.

ghost_open.patch now places a THIRD guard in do_open(), about fifteen lines
above this one, on write-intent and O_CREAT opens. The two do not overlap
textually and apply in either order. With both applied the earlier guard answers
open(p, O_DIRECTORY|O_WRONLY) first; this hunk stays as the narrower,
independently verified backstop for the read-only forms.

THE RCU TEST IS THE DESIGN WORK

ghost_hidden_path() renders d_path(), which needs a reference-counted path, and
-ENOTDIR can reach the end of path_lookupat() from sites that never unlazied:
path_init() rejects a non-directory dfd or a non-directory preset root with
`nd->flags` already carrying LOOKUP_RCU, before rcu_read_lock() and before
nd->path is assigned. So the guard tests `!(nd->flags & LOOKUP_RCU)` AND
`nd->path.dentry` before touching the predicate.

Both of those sites are unreachable-by-construction for the oracles here (they
fire before the walk starts, on the dfd, not on the name), so nothing is lost:
link_path_walk()'s site calls try_to_unlazy()/unlazy_walk() and returns -ECHILD
if it cannot, and complete_walk() runs ahead of the LOOKUP_DIRECTORY test. Every
probe listed at the top reaches the predicate in ref-walk mode. do_open() needs
no such test -- complete_walk() ran inside open_last_lookups() before it.

APPLIES TO

One file, 5.10 through 6.12. Both pre-images were counted in every tree before
this was regenerated: the path_lookupat() one is unique with `*path = nd->path;`
included, and do_open()'s `if ((nd->flags & LOOKUP_DIRECTORY) && ...)` is unique
on its own (path_lookupat() spells its test `if (!err && nd->flags & ...)`).

Verified with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` on the pinned OnePlus trees for 5.10,
5.15, 6.1, 6.6 and 6.12, and then APPLIED for real on each and the landing
function asserted by name. fs/namei.o compiles clean on all five
(ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1). Generated by editing the real file and running `diff -u4 -p`,
never by hand.

NOT covered: 4.9 / 4.14 / 4.19 / 5.4, which are not in the builder fleet and
have no tree here to verify against.
